# Analysing a Castor EDC study with Dux

```elixir
# To use checkouts instead of the published packages, set
# CASTOR_EDC_DUX_PATH, CASTOR_EDC_KINO_PATH and CASTOR_EDC_PATH.
dux = System.get_env("CASTOR_EDC_DUX_PATH")
kino = System.get_env("CASTOR_EDC_KINO_PATH")
core = System.get_env("CASTOR_EDC_PATH")

Mix.install(
  [
    if(dux, do: {:ex_castor_edc_dux, path: dux}, else: {:ex_castor_edc_dux, "~> 0.2.0"}),
    if(kino, do: {:ex_castor_edc_kino, path: kino}, else: {:ex_castor_edc_kino, "~> 0.1.0"}),
    {:kino_dux, "~> 0.2.0"}
  ] ++ if(core, do: [{:ex_castor_edc, path: core, override: true}], else: [])
)
```

## Introduction

This notebook loads a study's data into Dux frames, backed by DuckDB, and takes a first look at it.
The data goes to Parquet files as it streams in from the API, so it never has to fit in memory, and every frame's column types come from the study's structure.

## Connecting

Add your API credentials as Livebook secrets: `CASTOR_EDC_CLIENT_ID` and `CASTOR_EDC_CLIENT_SECRET`, which you can create on your [account settings page](https://data.castoredc.com/account/settings).
The connection cell below reads them from there, so they never appear in the notebook: pick the server your studies are on, and test the connection.

<!-- livebook:{"attrs":"","chunks":null,"kind":"Elixir.CastorEDC.Kino.ConnectionCell","livebook_object":"smart_cell"} -->

```elixir
client =
  CastorEDC.new(
    client_id: System.fetch_env!("LB_CASTOR_EDC_CLIENT_ID"),
    client_secret: System.fetch_env!("LB_CASTOR_EDC_CLIENT_SECRET"),
    base_url: "https://data.castoredc.com"
  )
```

Then pick the study to look at; the cell lists the studies the client can reach.

<!-- livebook:{"attrs":"eyJjbGllbnQiOiJjbGllbnQiLCJzdHVkeV9pZCI6IiIsInN0dWR5X25hbWUiOiIiLCJ2YXJpYWJsZSI6InN0dWR5X2lkIn0","chunks":null,"kind":"Elixir.CastorEDC.Kino.StudyCell","livebook_object":"smart_cell"} -->

```elixir

```

The frames' files go in one directory; remove it when you're done.

```elixir
dir = Path.join(System.tmp_dir!(), "castor_edc_#{study_id}")
```

## The study's structure

`fields/3` has a row per field that holds values, in the order the study shows them: where it collects data, its type, and its option group.
`option_groups/3` has a row per option, to join on `option_group_id`, though `data_points/3` can give the options' names itself (see below).

```elixir
{:ok, fields} = CastorEDC.Dux.fields(client, study_id, dir: dir)
{:ok, options} = CastorEDC.Dux.option_groups(client, study_id, dir: dir)

fields
|> Dux.compute()
```

## Participants and sites

```elixir
{:ok, participants} = CastorEDC.Dux.participants(client, study_id, dir: dir)
{:ok, sites} = CastorEDC.Dux.sites(client, study_id, dir: dir)

participants
|> Dux.join(sites |> Dux.select([:site_id, :name]) |> Dux.compute(), on: :site_id, how: :left)
|> Dux.group_by(:name)
|> Dux.summarise_with(participants: "COUNT(participant_id)", mean_progress: "AVG(progress)")
|> Dux.compute()
```

## Collected data

`participant_data/3` runs a batch export and queries its CSV files directly: a frame for the study's values, with a row per participant, and one for each repeating data and survey, with a row per instance.
It takes a few requests, however big the study is.

```elixir
{:ok, %{study: study, repeating_data: repeating_data, surveys: surveys}} =
  CastorEDC.Dux.participant_data(client, study_id, dir: dir, missing_columns: true)

study
|> Dux.compute()
```

Values marked missing are nulls, with their codes (95 to 99) in the `<variable>_missing` columns.

```elixir
Map.keys(repeating_data) ++ Map.keys(surveys)
```

An export you already downloaded reads the same way, without running another: pass the `CastorEDC.Export` struct, or the path to its zip or the directory it was unzipped into, as `from:`.
Unzipped files are read where they are; a zip goes into `<dir>/participant_data` first.

```elixir
{:ok, export} = CastorEDC.Export.participant_data(client, study_id, format: :csv)

{:ok, %{study: downloaded}} =
  CastorEDC.Dux.participant_data(client, study_id, from: export, dir: Path.join(dir, "downloaded"))

Dux.n_rows(downloaded)
```

`data_points/3` reads the same values through the API instead, page by page, with a row per value, which suits filtering and pivoting.
With `labels: true`, a `label` column holds the names of the chosen options, as the user saw them, next to their values:

```elixir
{:ok, values} = CastorEDC.Dux.data_points(client, study_id, labels: true, dir: dir)

values
|> Dux.filter_with("label IS NOT NULL")
|> Dux.group_by([:variable_name, :label])
|> Dux.summarise_with(count: "COUNT(*)")
|> Dux.compute()
```

Values marked missing have their code in `missing`:

```elixir
values
|> Dux.filter_with("missing IS NOT NULL")
|> Dux.group_by([:variable_name, :missing])
|> Dux.summarise_with(count: "COUNT(*)")
|> Dux.compute()
```

With `format: :wide`, it gives the same shape as `participant_data/3`, with a column per variable; `labels: true` puts the options' names in its radio and dropdown columns.

## Domains

A study whose fields are mapped to domains, as in CDISC SDTM, can be exported grouped by domain.
`domains/3` runs that export and gives a frame per domain, keyed by its abbreviation, with a row per participant per visit, repeating data instance or survey; `labels: true` adds the options' names.

```elixir
{:ok, domains} = CastorEDC.Dux.domains(client, study_id, labels: true, missing_columns: true, dir: dir)

Map.new(domains, fn {domain, frame} -> {domain, Dux.n_rows(frame)} end)
```

## Surveys, repeating data and queries

`survey_package_instances/3` has a row per survey package sent to (or started for) a participant, with its progress and when it was sent and finished:

```elixir
{:ok, packages} = CastorEDC.Dux.survey_package_instances(client, study_id, dir: dir)

packages
|> Dux.group_by([:survey_package_name, :status])
|> Dux.summarise_with(instances: "COUNT(*)", mean_progress: "AVG(progress)")
|> Dux.compute()
```

`repeating_data_instances/3` has a row per repeating data instance, such as each adverse event; their values are in the frames above, by `instance_id`:

```elixir
{:ok, instances} = CastorEDC.Dux.repeating_data_instances(client, study_id, dir: dir)

instances
|> Dux.filter_with("NOT archived")
|> Dux.group_by(:repeating_data_name)
|> Dux.summarise_with(instances: "COUNT(*)", participants: "COUNT(DISTINCT participant_id)")
|> Dux.compute()
```

`queries/3` has a row per query raised on a value; join it with `fields/3` for the field's name:

```elixir
{:ok, queries} = CastorEDC.Dux.queries(client, study_id, dir: dir)

queries
|> Dux.join(fields |> Dux.select([:field_id, :variable_name]) |> Dux.compute(), on: :field_id, how: :left)
|> Dux.select([:participant_id, :variable_name, :status, :first_query_remark, :remarks, :created_on])
|> Dux.compute()
```

## Anything else

`from_structs/2` makes a frame from any other result of the client, a list, page or stream of its structs, such as the study's visits.
Its types come from the values, as nothing else describes them, and nested objects are left out.

```elixir
client
|> CastorEDC.Forms.stream_visits(study_id)
|> CastorEDC.Dux.from_structs(dir: dir)
|> Dux.compute()
```
